schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "What Is a Blockchain?"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Plain-language, no-hype introduction to blockchains via a "village
    notebook" analogy: many computers (nodes) keep identical copies of a
    record, batched into blocks that hash-chain to make history
    tamper-evident ("immutable"), removing the need to trust any single
    keeper ("trustless") and solving double-spending without a referee.
    Covers real regenerative/community examples (Grassroots Economics'
    Sarafu Network, Regen Network), an enterprise comparison (Walmart on
    Hyperledger Fabric), and an honest costs section (energy use differences
    between proof-of-work and proof-of-stake, permanence cutting both ways,
    and that blockchains record agreement but don't create it).
  audience: community organisers and growers new to crypto
  known_tensions:
    - "immutability is good for trust but unforgiving for mistakes (wrong entries/payments usually can't be undone)"
    - "'blockchain wastes energy' is true of proof-of-work chains like Bitcoin but false of proof-of-stake chains most regenerative projects actually use"
  related_concepts:
    - blockchain
    - proof-of-work-vs-proof-of-stake
  related_resources:
    - "Grassroots Economics / Sarafu Network"
    - Regen Network
    - "Walmart / Hyperledger Fabric case study"
    - Bankless Academy
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-is-blockchain.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
# - Ledger Academy explainer and Etherscan block explorer: named only in the
#   "Try This"/references sections as follow-up learning links, not central
#   to the article's own argument — not given standalone resource objects to
#   avoid inflating passing mentions; left as related_resources context on
#   this entry instead.
# - Bitcoin, Ethereum, Celo (the underlying chains themselves): treated as
#   part of the proof-of-work-vs-proof-of-stake concept-lineage object rather
#   than separate resource entries, since the article discusses them as
#   consensus-mechanism examples, not as tools/projects in their own right.
